{"traj_id": "deepmath-85161_2", "problem": "Given two normally distributed random variables, X and Y, where X has a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, and Y has a mean of 60 and a standard deviation of 12, with a correlation coefficient of -0.6, calculate the probability that \\(2X < 0.5Y\\).", "gold": "0.0018", "pred": "0.0018", "is_correct": true, "tier": "hard", "n_tokens": 3850, "meta_leak": [], "paragraphs": ["We need to compute P(2X < 0.5Y) for X ~ N(50, 10^2), Y ~ N(60, 12^2), correlation ρ = -0.6.", "Let me define the event: 2X < 0.5Y, which is equivalent to 4X < Y, or Y - 4X > 0.", "Let Z = Y - 4X. I need to find the distribution of Z.", "Since X and Y are jointly normal, any linear combination is normal.", "Mean of Z:\nE[Z] = E[Y] - 4E[X] = 60 - 4(50) = 60 - 200 = -140", "Variance of Z:\nVar(Z) = Var(Y) + 16Var(X) - 8Cov(X,Y)", "Cov(X,Y) = ρ σ_X σ_Y = (-0.6)(10)(12) = -72", "Var(Z) = 144 + 16(100) - 8(-72)\n= 144 + 1600 + 576\n= 2320", "Standard deviation of Z:\nσ_Z = √2320 = √(16 × 145) = 4√145", "Let me compute √145: 12^2 = 144, so √145 ≈ 12.0416\nσ_Z ≈ 4 × 12.0416 = 48.1663", "Now P(Z > 0) = P((Z - μ_Z)/σ_Z > (0 - μ_Z)/σ_Z) = P(Z' > (0 - (-140))/√2320) = P(Z' > 140/√2320)", "Compute 140/√2320:\n√2320 = √(16 × 145) = 4√145 ≈ 4 × 12.04159 = 48.16638", "140/48.16638 ≈ 2.9067", "So P(Z' > 2.9067) = 1 - Φ(2.9067)", "Looking up Φ(2.9067):\nΦ(2.90) ≈ 0.99813\nΦ(2.91) ≈ 0.99821", "Let me be more precise.\n\nActually, let me recalculate more carefully.", "140/√2320:\n2320 = 140²/2320? No.\n140² = 19600\n19600/2320 = 8.4483\nSo (140/√2320)² = 8.4483\n140/√2320 = √8.4483 ≈ 2.9067", "So z = 2.9067\n\nΦ(2.9067):\nUsing standard normal table:\nΦ(2.90) = 0.99813\nΦ(2.91) = 0.99821", "Interpolating: Φ(2.9067) ≈ 0.99813 + 0.67 × (0.99821 - 0.99813) = 0.99813 + 0.67 × 0.00008 = 0.99813 + 0.0000536 ≈ 0.99818", "So P(Z > 0) = 1 - 0.99818 = 0.00182", "Hmm, let me double-check the calculation.\n\nActually, let me recompute the variance more carefully.", "Z = Y - 4X\n\nVar(Z) = Var(Y) + (-4)²Var(X) + 2(-4)Cov(Y,X)\n= Var(Y) + 16Var(X) - 8Cov(X,Y)", "Cov(X,Y) = ρ × σ_X × σ_Y = (-0.6) × 10 × 12 = -72", "Var(Z) = 144 + 16(100) - 8(-72)\n= 144 + 1600 + 576\n= 2320 ✓", "Mean of Z = 60 - 4(50) = -140 ✓", "z-score = (0 - (-140))/√2320 = 140/√2320", "Let me compute this more precisely:\n√2320: 48² = 2304, 48.1² = 2313.61, 48.16² = 2319.3856, 48.166² = 2319.963... \nLet me try: 48.1664² = 48.1664 × 48.1664", "Actually, 48² = 2304\n48.1² = 2313.61\n48.16² = 48.16 × 48.16 = 48 × 48.16 + 0.16 × 48.16 = 2311.68 + 7.7056 = 2319.3856\n48.17² = 48.17 × 48.17 = 48 × 48.17 + 0.17 × 48.17 = 2312.16 + 8.1889 = 2320.3489", "So √2320 is between 48.16 and 48.17.", "2320 - 2319.3856 = 0.6144\n2320.3489 - 2319.3856 = 0.9633", "So √2320 ≈ 48.16 + 0.6144/0.9633 × 0.01 ≈ 48.16 + 0.00638 ≈ 48.1664", "140/48.1664 ≈ 2.9067", "So P(Z > 0) = 1 - Φ(2.9067)", "Using more precise values:\nΦ(2.90) = 0.998134\nΦ(2.91) = 0.998208", "Interpolation: Φ(2.9067) ≈ 0.998134 + 0.67 × (0.998208 - 0.998134) = 0.998134 + 0.67 × 0.000074 = 0.998134 + 0.0000496 ≈ 0.998184", "P = 1 - 0.998184 = 0.001816", "So approximately 0.0018.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. The answer should be a clean number or expressed in terms of Φ.", "Actually, let me reconsider. The problem says \"calculate the probability.\" It might expect a numerical answer or an expression.", "Let me verify: z = 140/√2320", "Let me simplify: 140/√2320 = 140/(4√145) = 35/√145", "35/√145 = 35√145/145 = 7√145/29", "So z = 7√145/29\n\n7√145 = 7 × 12.04159 = 84.2911\n84.2911/29 = 2.9066", "So the probability is 1 - Φ(7√145/29) ≈ 0.0018", "Let me be more precise with the z-value:\n√145 = 12.041594578792...\n7 × 12.041594578792 = 84.291162051545\n84.291162051545 / 29 = 2.906591794881", "So z ≈ 2.9066\n\nΦ(2.9066):\nUsing more precise tables:\nΦ(2.90) = 0.9981342\nΦ(2.91) = 0.9982078", "Actually, let me use a more precise approach.\n\nThe standard normal CDF at z:\nΦ(z) = 0.5(1 + erf(z/√2))", "For z = 2.9066:\nz/√2 = 2.9066/1.41421 = 2.05529", "This is getting complicated. Let me just use the table values.\n\nΦ(2.90) = 0.99813\nΦ(2.91) = 0.99821", "For z = 2.9066:\nΦ(2.9066) ≈ 0.99813 + 0.66 × 0.00008 = 0.99813 + 0.0000528 = 0.99818", "P ≈ 1 - 0.99818 = 0.00182", "So the probability is approximately 0.0018.\n\nWait, let me reconsider whether the answer should be expressed as 1 - Φ(35/√145) or numerically.", "The problem says \"calculate the probability\" so I think a numerical answer is expected.", "Let me double-check my variance calculation:\nVar(Y - 4X) = Var(Y) - 8Cov(Y,X) + 16Var(X)\n= 144 - 8(-72) + 16(100)\n= 144 + 576 + 1600\n= 2320 ✓", "And the mean:\nE[Y - 4X] = 60 - 200 = -140 ✓", "So P(Y - 4X > 0) = P(Z > 0) where Z ~ N(-140, 2320)", "= P((Z + 140)/√2320 > 140/√2320)\n= P(N(0,1) > 2.9066)\n= 1 - Φ(2.9066)\n≈ 0.0018", "The answer is approximately 0.0018.\n\nLet me be even more precise. Using z = 2.9066:", "From standard normal tables with more precision:\nΦ(2.90) = 0.99813\nΦ(2.91) = 0.99821", "Actually, let me use the formula more carefully.\n\nFor z = 2.9066:\nThe linear interpolation between 2.90 and 2.91:\nfraction = 0.66\nΦ(2.9066) ≈ 0.99813 + 0.66 × (0.99821 - 0.99813)\n= 0.99813 + 0.66 × 0.00008\n= 0.99813 + 0.0000528\n= 0.9981828", "P = 1 - 0.9981828 = 0.0018172", "So approximately 0.0018.\n\nHmm, but maybe I should express it more precisely. Let me see if 140/√2320 simplifies nicely.", "140/√2320 = 140/√(16·145) = 140/(4√145) = 35/√145", "35/√145: multiply by √145/√145 = 35√145/145 = 7√145/29", "So z = 7√145/29\n\nThis doesn't simplify to a nice number.", "I'll go with the numerical answer of approximately 0.0018.", "Actually, let me reconsider if I should round differently. \n\nz ≈ 2.9066", "Looking at standard normal table:\nz = 2.90: Φ = 0.9981\nz = 2.91: Φ = 0.9982", "So 1 - Φ ≈ 0.0018 to 0.0019", "More precisely, 1 - Φ(2.9066) ≈ 0.0018\n\nI'll go with 0.0018."], "answer_text": "# Finding P(2X < 0.5Y) for Jointly Normal Variables\n\n## Setting Up the Linear Combination\n\nThe inequality $2X < 0.5Y$ is equivalent to $Y - 4X > 0$. I'll define:\n\n$$Z = Y - 4X$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are jointly normal, $Z$ is also normally distributed.\n\n## Computing the Mean of Z\n\n$$\\mu_Z = E[Y] - 4E[X] = 60 - 4(50) = 60 - 200 = -140$$\n\n## Computing the Variance of Z\n\n$$\\text{Var}(Z) = \\text{Var}(Y) + 16\\,\\text{Var}(X) - 8\\,\\text{Cov}(X,Y)$$\n\nFirst, the covariance:\n$$\\text{Cov}(X,Y) = \\rho \\cdot \\sigma_X \\cdot \\sigma_Y = (-0.6)(10)(12) = -72$$\n\nThen:\n$$\\text{Var}(Z) = 144 + 16(100) - 8(-72) = 144 + 1600 + 576 = 2320$$\n\nSo $\\sigma_Z = \\sqrt{2320} = 4\\sqrt{145} \\approx 48.166$\n\n## Standardizing and Computing the Probability\n\n$$P(Z > 0) = P\\!\\left(\\frac{Z - \\mu_Z}{\\sigma_Z} > \\frac{0 - (-140)}{\\sqrt{2320}}\\right) = P\\!\\left(Z' > \\frac{140}{\\sqrt{2320}}\\right)$$\n\nComputing the z-score:\n\n$$z = \\frac{140}{\\sqrt{2320}} = \\frac{35}{\\sqrt{145}} = \\frac{7\\sqrt{145}}{29} \\approx 2.9066$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$P(Z > 0) = 1 - \\Phi(2.9066)$$\n\n## Looking Up the Standard Normal CDF\n\nUsing standard normal tables with interpolation between $\\Phi(2.90) = 0.99813$ and $\\Phi(2.91) = 0.99821$:\n\n$$\\Phi(2.9066) \\approx 0.99813 + 0.66(0.00008) = 0.99818$$\n\n$$P(2X < 0.5Y) = 1 - 0.99818 \\approx 0.00182$$\n\n$$\\boxed{0.0018}$$", "annotations": {"GPT-6.1·default": {"nodes": [{"node_id": 0, "p_start": 0, "p_end": 2, "label": "SU", "deps": [], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 1, "p_start": 3, "p_end": 3, "label": "RC", "deps": [0], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 2, "p_start": 4, "p_end": 4, "label": "CP", "deps": [0], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 3, "p_start": 5, "p_end": 5, "label": "RC", "deps": [0], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 4, "p_start": 6, "p_end": 9, "label": "CP", "deps": [0, 3], "branch": 0, "path": "M", "flags": ""}, {"node_id": 5, "p_start": 10, "p_end": 13, "label": "CP", "deps": [1, 2, 4], "branch": 0, "